The exits module
================

    >>> from pysyte.cli import exits
    >>> assert "handle exit codes" in exits.__doc__

    >>> from pysyte import os


Mirrors os.EX_...
-----------------

The exists module provides equivalents for the "os.EX_..." constants

    >>> assert int(exits.ok) == os.EX_OK
    >>> assert str(exits.ok) == "EX_OK"

Note that the module read is pysyte's "os" module
    which enhances python's "os" module, e.g. it adds "EX_FAIL"

And that value has also been captured by the exits module:
    >>> assert exits.fail == os.EX_FAIL

Usage
-----

Taking "EX_USAGE" as an example

It has led to creation of a module object called "usage"
    >>> usage = exits.usage
    >>> assert usage == os.EX_USAGE

That can be treated as in int
    >>> i = int(usage)
    >>> assert i == usage.code
    >>> assert i == os.EX_USAGE

Or as a string
    >>> s = str(usage)
    >>> assert usage.NAME == "EX_USAGE" == s
    >>> assert usage.name == "usage"

Exit program
------------

Calling the exit() method raises the relevant SystemExit exception

    >>> try:
    ...     usage.exit()
    ...     assert False, "Test failed, no exception"
    ... except SystemExit as e:
    ...     assert e.code[0] == os.EX_USAGE
    ... except Exception as e:
    ...     assert False, "Test failed, wrong exception {e=}"
